{
  "text": "The lifetime claim holds for this callsite. `new marked.Lexer({ ...marked.defaults, tokenizer: undefined })` passes an object that is not `marked.defaults`. The installed constructor treats `tokenizer: undefined` as missing (`options.tokenizer || new Tokenizer`) and writes that new tokenizer, `tokenizer.options`, and `tokenizer.lexer = this` only onto the fresh pair. `inlineQueue` is the lexer’s own `[]`, so `lexer.inlineQueue.length = 0` touches this invocation alone. The cycle is lexer → options → tokenizer → lexer, and nothing in that sequence assigns the tokenizer back onto `marked.defaults`. If `blockTokens` throws, the queue clear is skipped and the cycle is reachable only from the dying frame. The shared-root failure mode is gone on this path, including the throw path. `blockTokens` fills `type` and `raw` before any inline drain, so clearing the queue leaves those fields intact.\n\nNo separate production correctness defect follows from this patch. Stock block rules are the shared non-global module tables, so this path does not leave a `lastIndex`. The shallow copy still shares `extensions` and `hooks`; with both null and no `marked.use` or `marked.options` in the repo, that sharing has no effect.\n\nThe regression is its own process-lifetime root. `other = new marked.Lexer()` still installs `other`’s tokenizer on `marked.defaults`, and its `blockTokens` call leaves that queue undrained on purpose. The assertions detect retargeting of that shared tokenizer, and after the test `marked.defaults.tokenizer.lexer` still points at `other`. A later bare `new marked.Lexer()` or `marked.lexer()` in the same worker reuses it.\n\nThis covers the shown constructor, the queue clear, and the regression, assuming the whole-text fallback uses the same detached construction. A fallback that still calls the bare constructor would root that fallback lexer on `marked.defaults`. CRLF normalization, block parity, and skipping WASM are unchanged.",
  "stopReason": "end_turn",
  "sessionId": "01a1a0bc-9050-7622-9ec5-8516e1fc7805",
  "requestId": "0065e643-7f2e-4b11-9241-a34e4e19aa67",
  "cwd": "/Users/akiralam/code/ax-code-desktop",
  "thought": "Preparing a final focused review in English, under 350 words, with no tools or edits.\n\nRe-examining the marked.js Lexer isolation claim. The patch uses fresh Lexer instances with cached normalized text and offsets. Earlier reviewers approved this for queue isolation. Local verification shows that options and tokenizer are shared across instances. This raises a concrete correctness issue: a fresh lexer does not actually isolate its queue.\n\nThe shared tokenizer holds a lexer backreference, so its last undrained queue can retain text globally. The concrete fix is to pass `tokenizer: undefined` in the Lexer constructor and explicitly clear `lexer.inlineQueue.length = 0` after blockTokens.\n\nThe public constructor stores a lexer backreference on any tokenizer it creates. Passing `tokenizer: undefined` creates an invocation-owned tokenizer that is discarded on throw. The fallback cycle is therefore local and cannot leak globally. Production code only consumes type and raw fields, and no global marked.use or options mutations exist in this repository.\n\nA regression test now snapshots another lexer's inlineQueue and asserts its tokenizer backreference and pending queue remain unchanged after the production builder runs. This validates that the isolated lexer cannot leak state into shared options or tokenizer objects.\n\n`blockTokens` defers inline parsing into `inlineQueue`. The queue retains references to source text slices. Clearing its length drops those references so the large normalized text can be garbage collected.\n\nThe shallow options copy isolates the tokenizer from marked.defaults, but nested mutable objects in defaults remain shared. If the lexer mutates those objects, the change persists globally.\n\nThe shallow options copy is safe for most fields. `extensions`, `hooks`, `walkTokens`, and `renderer` are either null or immutable, so they cannot leak state. The constructor writes `tokenizer.rules` onto the new tokenizer rather than on shared defaults. `blockTokens` mutates only lexer-local properties such as `this.state`, `this.tokens`, and `this.inlineQueue`. The remaining risk is that a prior `marked.use()` call could have mutated `defaults.extensions`, and the spread would then copy that same mutable reference.\n\nThe repository-local claim that no global `marked.use` or `marked.options` mutations exist is a residual assumption. If a dependency mutates defaults as a side effect of import, extensions could still leak state.\n\nPassing `tokenizer: undefined` in the constructor explicitly overwrites any tokenizer reference copied from `marked.defaults`. This prevents a stale shared instance from leaking into the fresh lexer.\n\nThe `options.tokenizer || new Tokenizer()` pattern is unchanged in marked v4 and v5. The concrete correctness issue is therefore not a version-specific regression.\n\nIf an exception occurs before `inlineQueue.length = 0`, the local lexer retains its queue entries. The user claims that on throw, the whole-text fallback is retained and no shared root holds this local cycle.\n\nIf the throw occurs inside blockTokens, the local lexer becomes unreachable once the function exits. The only references are the local const and the tokenizer backreference, so the cycle should be garbage collected. The sole remaining leak path is if marked.defaults or another shared structure captured the lexer.\n\nMarked 9+ exposes `marked.options` as a live binding, so mutating defaults mutates the lexer options object directly. This makes the shallow options copy less effective than assumed. The constructor also assigns tokenizer rules after instantiation, which can mutate shared state.\n\nThe text tokenizer pushes a `{src, tokens}` object onto the inlineQueue. The `tokens` field is the same token object already returned in the tokens array. Clearing the queue length removes those entries. The returned tokens still hold `text` and `raw` as source slices. Production code only consumes `type` and `raw`. This is expected behavior.\n\nA new concern is the lifetime of the normalized text string. `slice` may share a backing store with the original in some engines, and V8 can retain the parent string via SlicedString until it flattens. If production keeps tokens, those tokens' `raw` fields are substrings of that sliced string, so the parent normalized text could remain reachable even after the lexer exits.\n\nThe normalized text slicing concern is a memory lifetime issue, not a correctness defect. The user explicitly excluded end-to-end speed claims.\n\nClearing `inlineQueue.length = 0` does not alter the returned tokens array, since queue entries reference those same token objects.\n\nChecking whether `blockTokens` mutates `lexer.tokens` directly or only returns them as a value.\n\nThe production code calls `blockTokens` directly rather than through `lex()`. This means the original CRLF normalization remains intact, because `blockTokens` itself does not normalize line endings.\n\nA concrete semantic parity risk remains. `blockTokens` never drains `inlineQueue`, so paragraph and text tokens may lack inline token children. If production code reads `token.tokens` or `token.text`, it will diverge from the original `marked.lexer()` behavior, which fully parses inline content.\n\nThe missing inline token children are a pre-existing design of the earlier approved change, not introduced by the isolation fix. Spreading `marked.defaults` copies current enumerable properties at call time, so it is safe. Some marked versions invoke `this.options.hooks` during lex, which may affect `blockTokens`.\n\nI checked the marked 14 and 15 source for `Lexer.blockTokens` and the constructor. The constructor normalizes tabs to spaces, which is a semantic parity risk if production code relies on the original tab behavior.\n\nThe constructor assigns `this.options.tokenizer` to a fresh `Tokenizer(this.options)` instance, then overwrites `this.tokenizer` with that new reference. It also sets `this.tokenizer.lexer = this`, binding the lexer to its own tokenizer. This confirms the isolated lexer owns both its tokenizer and inline queue.\n\nThe constructor assigns tokenizer rules after instantiation, which can mutate shared state. The Tokenizer constructor accepts options and sets this.options directly.\n\nThe constructor overwrites `this.tokenizer.options`, `this.tokenizer.lexer`, and `this.tokenizer.rules` onto the fresh instance. Spreading `marked.defaults` cannot leak a stale tokenizer because `tokenizer: undefined` is passed explicitly; the constructor then instantiates a new Tokenizer on the isolated copy, leaving shared defaults untouched.\n\nThe spread of `marked.defaults` is safe because `tokenizer` is a normal property. A concrete correctness issue was identified: assigning tokenizer rules copies module-level regex objects that carry `/g` or `/y` flags and mutable `lastIndex`. This is a classic marked bug where shared regex state leaks across lexer instances.\n\nThe isolated Tokenizer still receives the same module-level regex objects. A throw mid-match can leave `lastIndex` non-zero on those shared singletons, poisoning subsequent parses globally. This is a correctness issue, but it concerns regex state rather than the queue lifetime claim.\n\nThe isolated lexer fixes the prior global inlineQueue leak. Marked's block regexes are non-global, so they do not carry mutable lastIndex state. The shared regex poison concern is therefore limited to inline rules.\n\nSince the code path only calls blockTokens, inline rules are never executed and regex lastIndex pollution is irrelevant here. After a successful run, clearing inlineQueue.length = 0 releases its items, leaving the lexer object graph with only local references.\n\nThe success path is fully collectable. The lexer object graph has no shared root once the function returns. On throw, the local lexer retains its queue and source substrings until stack unwind drops the reference; the cycle then becomes orphaned and collectable. The lifetime claim holds only if the constructor does not write the tokenizer back to marked.defaults.\n\nThe constructor does not write the tokenizer back to marked.defaults. The spread breaks identity, so the lifetime claim is now correct.\n\nA residual assumption remains: the shallow copy still shares extensions, hooks, and renderer if any dependency mutates marked defaults at import time. This is conditional rather than a concrete defect.\n\nThe regression test constructs a fresh `marked.Lexer()` without options. The constructor mutates `marked.defaults` by setting `defaults.tokenizer` when it is null. This means the test itself alters global state, which invalidates its own isolation check.\n\nThe regression test pollutes global defaults before invoking production, yet the isolated copy still prevents leakage. The test only verifies that the fresh lexer retains its own tokenizer reference; it does not prove that production never mutates marked.defaults.\n\nThe regression test actually catches two forms of global mutation. Because the fresh lexer was constructed without options, its `options` object is `marked.defaults`. The assertion `other.options.tokenizer.lexer === other` therefore fails if production replaces `defaults.tokenizer` with a new instance whose `.lexer` points to the production lexer. It also fails if production mutates the existing shared tokenizer's `.lexer` reference. The test therefore detects both direct replacement and in-place mutation of the shared defaults tokenizer.\n\nThe regression test snapshots `inlineQueue` and verifies it is unchanged. That queue lives on the Lexer instance, not the tokenizer. The shared leak was caused by a shared tokenizer whose `.lexer` field pointed to the last lexer, which then held text in its own inlineQueue.\n\nA concrete correctness issue was found in the prefix-stability logic. Slicing the normalized text at `stablePrefixLength` can land inside a multi-byte character boundary, causing the suffix to tokenize differently than if the entire document were parsed from the start. This is a pre-existing design flaw rather than a regression introduced by the current patch.\n\nThe slice is part of the current patch, not a pre-existing flaw. Markdown block parsing is not prefix-stable: later tokens such as link reference definitions or setext headings can alter earlier inline parsing. Therefore the suffix may tokenize differently than if the entire document were parsed from the start.\n\nMissing link references in the stable prefix are harmless because production only consumes type and raw fields. Paragraph merging across the boundary is safe if the prefix ends at a completed block.\n\nThe spread copies silent, gfm, breaks, and pedantic, so behavior matches the call-time defaults. Hooks remain unchanged because blockTokens does not invoke them. A concrete issue was noted but left incomplete.\n\nThe spread still shares the `extensions` array if defaults already holds one. `marked.use` can mutate that array or replace the tokenizer on defaults, but the repository contains no such call. This is a bounded assumption rather than a concrete defect.\n\nI cannot verify whether the local marked version handles `tokenizer: undefined` differently from the reviewed code.\n\nThe installed marked constructor matches the reviewed pattern, so the fix is correct for that version. Object spread copies function-valued properties like walkTokens and renderer, but blockTokens does not invoke them, so mutation of those fields is harmless.\n\nThe `blockTokens` method may copy `this.tokens.links` into the returned array. If the caller uses that return value instead of `lexer.tokens`, link references could leak across lexer instances.\n\nThe `blockTokens` method returns a fresh array when no tokens argument is passed, so link references cannot leak across lexer instances. Production only consumes type and raw fields from that returned array.\n\nTab expansion inside `blockTokens` mutates the source string locally. The sliced suffix is a separate string, so tab expansion results stay isolated. `token.raw` comes from the expanded source, which matches full lex behavior. A tab sequence split across the slice boundary could expand differently.\n\nThe local lexer graph is fully self-contained. The tokenizer is stored only on the fresh options object, and the lexer holds no external reference to that options object. No other code path can retain the cycle once the function returns.\n\nTokenizer rules are regex literals, not closures over the lexer, so no shared root can be created by mutating them. The options object is local to the fresh instance, and returned tokens do not reference the lexer, leaving the cycle fully orphaned once the function returns.\n\nThe catch block falls back to whole-text lexing and discards the local lexer. The thrown error contains no reference to that lexer, so the lifetime claim remains valid even on failure. The async option does not affect blockTokens because it is synchronous.\n\nThe regression test permanently couples `marked.defaults` to the fresh lexer instance. Later tests that construct a new `marked.Lexer()` without options inherit that shared tokenizer, so the test pollutes global state for subsequent runs.\n\nThe regression test itself creates a global lifetime leak by anchoring a lexer on `marked.defaults.tokenizer.lexer` for the rest of the process. This is a test-induced artifact, not a production defect. It is unclear whether this qualifies as a concrete correctness issue in the final change.\n\nThe regression test is a test hygiene issue. It permanently installs the fresh lexer's tokenizer onto `marked.defaults`, so later tests that construct a bare `new marked.Lexer()` without options inherit that shared tokenizer and overwrite its `lexer` reference. This makes the suite order-dependent.\n\nThe regression test itself re-creates the global coupling that the patch removes. If a later test assumes `defaults.tokenizer` is null, it will fail because the fixture leaves a stale tokenizer installed on defaults. The test sequence also shows that production can create a fresh tokenizer whose `.lexer` points to the production lexer while leaving the original shared tokenizer on defaults untouched.\n\nThe regression test assertion is sound. It correctly detects if production retargets the shared tokenizer's lexer reference. The leftover global state is a test isolation footgun, not a false pass.\n\nClearing the inlineQueue after blockTokens returns does not strip token.raw values. Consumers only need type and raw fields, so this ordering introduces no correctness loss.\n\nA concrete correctness issue was found in the offset caching logic. Tab expansion inside `blockTokens` can change the source string length, so cached offsets into the normalized text will drift when slicing the suffix. This breaks the alignment between token raw lengths and their corresponding source positions.\n\nThe tab-expansion offset drift is a pre-existing issue. Full-text blockTokens also expands tabs before slicing raw, so position mapping was already broken in expanded space. This is not a regression introduced by the current patch.\n\nTab expansion is line-local, so slicing at a newline boundary preserves parity with full-document tokenization. A mid-line cut could alter the expansion pattern. The offset cache must be line-based to avoid drift.\n\nI will not invent a prefix-rule bug without confirming it is unsafe. I am now examining the concrete lexer construction itself.\n\nThe marked source confirms `defaults` is a plain object with only enumerable properties. Spreading it therefore copies every field cleanly, and the explicit `tokenizer: undefined` override is safe.\n\nA prior marked GitHub issue confirms the exact shared-tokenizer bug. The recommended fix is to pass a fresh defaults object to the constructor, such as `new marked.Lexer({ ...marked.getDefaults() })`, rather than spreading `marked.defaults` directly.\n\n`getDefaults()` returns a fresh defaults object, but if `marked.defaults` is a getter that returns the live shared instance, spreading it would copy any already-polluted tokenizer. The explicit `tokenizer: undefined` override prevents this in both cases.\n\nSpreading `marked.defaults` preserves live user configuration from `marked.use` and `setOptions`, while `getDefaults()` would reset to factory defaults and discard it. The `hooks` object is shared by reference, but `blockTokens` does not invoke hooks, so this poses no correctness risk.\n\nThe fallback path may still invoke the old `marked.lexer(text)` or `new marked.Lexer().blockTokens(full)`. That would recreate the global leak on the error path. The user only guarantees the throw path of the new isolated lexer is free of shared roots. They do not confirm the fallback uses the fresh constructor.\n\nThe fallback path mutates `marked.defaults` directly. It installs the fresh tokenizer and leaves its `lexer` field pointing at the fallback lexer instance. That fallback instance retains the full token tree in `this.tokens` for the lifetime of the shared defaults object. This is the original leak, but it only affects the fallback path. Whether this qualifies as a concrete correctness issue in the final change is still being weighed.\n\nThe user's claim that no shared root retains the local cycle applies to the new isolated lexer, not the fallback path. The fallback is a separate code path that may still leak globally, but it is outside the scope of this change.\n\nThe fallback path may still leak globally if it uses the unpatched constructor. The user's claim that no shared root retains the local cycle is being re-evaluated against this possibility.\n\nThe fallback path is a conditional risk: it only leaks globally if it still uses the bare constructor. The lifetime claim holds for the isolated lexer itself. I will list only the concrete issues I am confident about, and I am now re-examining whether `tokenizer: null` versus `undefined` in the spread is safe, since the constructor installs a tokenizer via `options.tokenizer || new Tokenizer`.\n\nThe explicit `tokenizer: undefined` override is safe. Spreading `marked.defaults` first preserves any existing tokenizer, then the override forces the fresh constructor to create a new one via `undefined || new Tokenizer`. I will not speculate about Tokenizer constructor side effects.\n\nA concrete correctness issue was found in how rules are assigned. The code sets `this.tokenizer.rules = rules`, where `rules.block` is the shared rules module. This mutates the global rules object directly, so later lexers that share the same rules module will inherit any mutations made by this lexer.\n\nThe rules mutation concern is resolved. No block regex in the shared module is global, so resetting `lastIndex` on any fence pattern is harmless. The marked 5.0+ constructor also accepts a full options object directly, confirming that spreading `marked.defaults` produces a valid configuration.\n\nThe inlineQueue clearing is safe. Tokens are pushed by reference, and truncating the queue does not mutate their fields.\n\nParagraph continuation updates mutate token.src and token.text, but clearing the inlineQueue afterward is safe because those values are already final. The lifetime claim for the production constructor is correct. A shallow copy of nested extensions or hooks remains a low risk only if a dependency later calls marked.use, which is not shown.\n\nA concrete test-isolation defect was found in the focused tests. They leave `marked.defaults.tokenizer.lexer` pointing at the fresh lexer, retaining that instance's queue for the process lifetime. The tests also fail to restore the original tokenizer afterward, and they do not verify that a later bare `new marked.Lexer()` overwrites the shared field. This is a correctness issue in the final change.\n\nThe inlineQueue clearing is safe. A slice at a non-block boundary could alter tokenization, but the parity checks are meant to catch that. The shallow spread is not a risk if marked.defaults.tokenizer is not a getter with side effects. The current focus is whether blockTokens pushes tokens into this.tokens while also returning them, which would mean truncating inlineQueue does not fully clear the queue.\n\nThe `blockTokens` method accepts a fresh array as its default `tokens` argument, so it does not mutate the lexer's own `this.tokens` field. This confirms that truncating `inlineQueue` after tokenization is safe.\n\nThe tokenizer may retain the last regex match via `RegExp.lastMatch` or `lastIndex` on the shared block regexes. This is a concrete correctness issue in the final change.\n\nThe patch leaves a concrete lifetime hole. `RegExp.input`, `RegExp.lastMatch`, and other static regex properties retain the last matched input string globally until the next regex exec anywhere in the isolate. The new lexer does not clear these statics, so they leak across calls.\n\nThe concrete lifetime leak is weaker than it first appeared. `RegExp.input` retains only the last matched suffix, not the full document, because block tokenizers try rules sequentially and the final successful match is typically a small remainder. The earlier concern about global retention of the entire source string is overstated.\n\nThe regex static retention is pre-existing and engine-dependent, so it does not qualify as a concrete bug introduced by this change. Truncating the inlineQueue is harmless; it only shortens the retained graph before garbage collection.\n\nThe marked 8+ tokenizer stores a singleton `block.normal` rules object. If an extension or tokenizer mutates `rules.block._lexer`, that shared reference would leak across lexers.\n\nThe shallow spread of `marked.defaults` is necessary to preserve the `gfm` flag. Passing only `{ tokenizer: undefined }` would leave `this.options.gfm` undefined, so GFM rules would not be applied.\n\nThe shallow spread of `marked.defaults` does not deep-clone `extensions`. The constructor may mutate `this.options.extensions.renderers` or `startBlock` during initialization, so a later lexer sharing the same extensions object could inherit those mutations.\n\nThe constructor mutates the caller's options object by assigning `this.options.tokenizer` to the fresh tokenizer. A shallow copy of `marked.defaults` therefore does not prevent this mutation. The remaining risk is whether nested objects inside that copied options, such as extensions or renderers, are written to during lexer construction.\n\nThe regression assertion is a concrete test-isolation defect. It only verifies that `other.options.tokenizer.lexer` still points to the fresh lexer instance, but it does not check whether a later bare `new marked.Lexer()` overwrites that shared field. If production still uses the old constructor path, the global leak would remain undetected.\n\nThe regression test catches a tokenizer leak because `other.options` is the same object as `marked.defaults`. A production leak through an unrelated property such as `hooks` would evade detection, but the patch does not suggest that path.\n\nThe regression test intentionally leaves a pending global queue containing `'Other **message**'` by calling `other.blockTokens`. This is the canary meant to catch production leaks. The test itself pollutes the process state afterward, which is a minor issue.\n\nThe stable-prefix slice is safe. The cached offsets were computed on the normalized text, so they remain valid after CRLF removal.\n\nThe tokenizer options cycle is local. The fresh options object breaks the shared reference to marked.defaults, and tokenizer: undefined forces a fresh Tokenizer whose lexer backreference is invocation-owned. The inlineQueue is per-instance, so clearing it cannot affect other lexers.\n\nIf the lexer throws before clearing the inlineQueue, the tokenizer cycle dies with the stack frame and no shared root is retained. The fallback path remains a concern only if it still uses bare `new Lexer()` or `marked.lexer()`, which lies outside the shown snippet.\n\nThe regression test reintroduces the global root after production stops creating it. A later bare `new marked.Lexer()` call in the same worker then shares that tokenizer, so the test pollution is a concrete test-isolation defect.\n\nThe `tokenizer: undefined` override is safe because the application does not register a custom tokenizer via `marked.use`. A pre-existing concern remains that `blockTokens` omits inline tokenization, so raw values may diverge from those produced by the full lexer.\n\nThe marked 15.0 Lexer constructor assigns `this.options = options || defaults` directly, so the shallow spread of `marked.defaults` is required to preserve the `gfm` flag and other default settings. Without it, production would lose those defaults and apply incorrect rules.\n\nThe patch does not clear pre-existing global pollution from earlier bare constructors. It only stops this callsite from contributing to it. Other `new marked.Lexer()` or `marked.lexer()` calls in the same process would still leak, but the production builder is the sole reviewed path.\n\nThe regression test creates a fresh lexer, so if production uses only this callsite, the leak is contained. Tab-expansion and silent infinite-loop issues in `blockTokens` are pre-existing and not introduced by this change. The normalized suffix survives because `token.raw` retains it; this is required for correctness, not a leak. The prefix may be garbage-collected unless V8's sliced-string retention keeps the parent string alive.\n\nThe regression test can only verify the tokenizer cycle if no prior test already mutated `defaults.tokenizer`. If an earlier test left a shared tokenizer in that slot, the fresh lexer constructor keeps it and retargets its `lexer` back-reference to the new instance. The test then sees `other.options.tokenizer.lexer === other`, but this is a side effect of stale global state rather than proof that the cycle is truly local.\n\nThe regression assertion is sound because it checks the tokenizer cycle immediately after construction, before any production code can mutate the shared defaults. A pre-polluted global tokenizer would not break this test; it would only trigger a side effect. The constructor also assigns stateful singleton rules to a freshly created tokenizer, so cross-talk between lexers is impossible.\n\nThe constructor evaluates the right-hand side of `this.options.tokenizer = this.options.tokenizer || new Tokenizer()` before assigning the result. A freshly created Tokenizer therefore sees `undefined` for `options.tokenizer` during its own construction, so it cannot accidentally capture a pre-existing shared tokenizer.\n\nThe lifetime claim is sound. No concrete production correctness bug was found. The regression test leaves a minor test-isolation residue, and the fallback path remains a conditional caveat. The earlier approvals were wrong because they missed the shared defaults leak.\n\nThe `blockTokens` method does not introduce a new leak. A potential hole remains in marked 2 and 4: `marked.defaults` is a getter that returns a live binding to the internal `defaults` variable, so mutating it would affect every lexer instance. The patch does not guard against this.\n\nThe spread copy of `marked.defaults` is safe because the property is a live getter and setter. A marked 5 Instance defaults mismatch is irrelevant here since no `marked.use` is present. The review is now examining whether clearing `inlineQueue` in the fallback path could introduce a correctness bug.\n\nThe fallback path is safe. If `blockTokens` throws, the local lexer is garbage-collected and the fallback runs without issue. Custom extension tokens cannot hold a reference to the lexer. The review is now examining whether shared `tokens.links` could introduce a correctness bug.\n\nThe constructor initializes `this.tokens.links` as a null object, and marked 9's `blockTokens` copies that map into the output array when the array is empty. This shared link map could leak across lexer instances if the caller reuses the same tokens array.\n\nThe `def` tokenizer writes link references into `this.lexer.tokens.links`, not the `tokens` array passed to `blockTokens`. This confirms that shared link state lives on the lexer instance itself, so a reused tokens array cannot leak link definitions across separate lexers.\n\nThe fresh tokenizer also isolates `state.inRawBlock`, because state lives on the lexer instance rather than the tokenizer. This confirms the fix resolves a second facet of the same shared-tokenizer bug.\n\nThe shallow copy of `marked.defaults` still shares the `extensions` object if one is ever non-null. This is not currently a problem.\n\nThe tokenizer rules objects are also shared module-level references. If any block rule used a sticky or global regexp, its `lastIndex` could leak across lexers. The stock block rules are non-global, so this is not a concrete bug.\n\nThe regression test snapshots the inline queue after `blockTokens` and asserts it is unchanged. Production clears its own queue with `inlineQueue.length = 0`, so the test passes without detecting a leak. This creates false confidence that the fix is fully isolated.\n\nThe shallow spread of `marked.defaults` is confirmed safe because the installed package exposes a live binding that the spread detaches. No concrete production correctness defect was found in the reviewed change.\n\nThe fallback path is not a new logic bug; it was already present. Clearing `inlineQueue` on the success path is redundant for isolation because the queue was never shared, but it allows large suffix strings to be collected if anything retains the lexer.\n\nThe review is now probing whether the Tokenizer constructor itself mutates `marked.defaults` when it receives no arguments. If the constructor assigns `this.options.tokenizer = this` while `options` still points to the shared defaults object, the fresh lexer would publish its own tokenizer back into the global defaults. This would create a new leak channel that the shallow copy of `marked.defaults` does not prevent.\n\nThe Tokenizer constructor does not install itself onto `marked.defaults` when called without arguments. The Lexer constructor performs that assignment instead, so the fresh tokenizer is never published into global defaults.\n\nThe shallow spread of `marked.defaults` copies the `extensions` array by reference. If that array is non-null, a later mutation would leak across lexer instances. The current code leaves it null, so this is not a concrete bug.\n\nA concrete correctness issue remains in the sliced suffix logic. If `stablePrefixLength` falls inside a line that contains leading tabs, the tab-expansion regex in `blockTokens` sees a different line start than it would on the full string. This causes `raw` to diverge from a full-document lex. The cache must therefore yield offsets only at block boundaries that are also line boundaries.\n\nThe slice call was already approved as part of the earlier offset-caching design. The review will only flag the boundary precondition if it is confirmed to be unchecked.\n\nThe review will not center on the slice boundary issue because parity checks already cover semantic parity. The fallback path is confirmed safe: clearing `inlineQueue` does not break returned tokens, and it only runs if `blockTokens` throws. A test gap remains: the regression test never asserts that `marked.defaults.tokenizer` is the same reference before and after the call, though `other.options.tokenizer.lexer === other` implies this when `other.options === defaults`.\n\nThe regression test mutates global defaults by constructing a fresh `marked.Lexer()`. This leaves the test file’s tokenizer and its undrained inline queue alive for the worker lifetime. Later tests that reuse `marked.lexer` or `new Lexer()` will then share and overwrite that same tokenizer, producing observable cross-test state coupling. This is a concrete test-correctness footgun.\n\nThe lifetime claim holds. The fresh options object is not `marked.defaults`. The constructor installs the new tokenizer and its options onto that isolated pair. `inlineQueue` is allocated on the lexer instance, so clearing it only affects this invocation. The resulting cycle is lexer → options → tokenizer → lexer.\n\nThe regression test itself is a real process-lifetime root. It installs its tokenizer onto `marked.defaults` and leaves the inline queue undrained, so the same tokenizer object persists across subsequent tests.\n\nThe review assumes the whole-text fallback also uses the detached construction. If it still calls bare `new marked.Lexer()` or `marked.lexer()`, that fallback lexer would root on `marked.defaults` for the process lifetime.",
